{-
   A reader/writer mutex - many readers, or one writer.

   Worth having exactly where the read side dominates and the critical sections are long enough to
   pay for the extra state: a configuration map read on every request and written once a minute, a
   routing table, a cache index. For anything short or write-heavy a plain `Mutex` is faster, because
   a reader/writer lock's read path is still an atomic read-modify-write on one shared line and it
   has strictly more of them.

   § One word

       bit 31        a writer holds it
       bit 30        a writer is waiting
       bits 0..29    how many readers hold it

   Both halves in one word so that a reader's acquisition is a single compare-exchange - the
   alternative, a count and a flag in separate words, cannot be read consistently without a third
   thing to serialize them.

   § Writers are preferred, and that is a choice

   A reader will not take the lock while bit 30 is set. Without that, a stream of readers can hold
   the count above zero forever and a writer never runs - reader starvation is not a theoretical risk
   here, it is what a read-dominated workload *does*. The cost is that a writer's announcement stalls
   readers that would otherwise have proceeded, which is the trade this makes deliberately.

   It is not fair in any stronger sense: among writers, and among readers once a writer has gone,
   whoever the kernel starts first wins. A FIFO reader/writer lock needs a queue, and a queue is a
   different type.

   § Everyone waits on the same word

   One futex address, so a release wakes readers and writers alike and each re-checks. That is a
   thundering herd on a wide release, and the alternative - two addresses, so a write unlock can wake
   only readers - costs a second cache line and an argument about which to wake first. This is the
   simple version; the measurement that would justify the other one does not exist yet.
-}
import Native
import Atomic
import Native.Thread.Linux

let writerHeld = 0x80000000 :: U32
let writerWaiting = 0x40000000 :: U32
let readerMask = 0x3FFFFFFF :: U32

-- What a reader may not proceed past: the lock being held for writing, or a writer having announced
-- itself. Both in one constant because a reader tests them together and never separately.
let readerBlocked = 0xC0000000 :: U32

pub data RwMutex {state: Atomic(U32)}

pub fn rwMutex() -> RwMutex = RwMutex {state: atomic(0 :: U32)}

-- How many readers hold it, right now. Advisory, like every unsynchronized observation.
pub fn readerCount(self: RwMutex) -> U32 = self.state.load(LoadRelaxed) `and` readerMask

{-
   One read attempt, which never blocks.

   The compare-exchange carries the whole observed word rather than just the count, which is what
   makes it a single step: a reader that incremented only the low bits could do so between a writer
   setting bit 31 and that writer noticing, and would then be inside a lock somebody else holds.
-}
pub fn tryLockRead(self: RwMutex) -> Bool:
    let &observed = self.state.load(LoadRelaxed)

    while (observed `and` readerBlocked) == 0:
        match self.state.compareExchange(observed, observed + 1, UpdateAcquire):
            Exchanged -> return True
            Unchanged(->actual) -> observed = actual

    return False

-- Take it for reading, waiting while a writer holds it or has announced itself. The expectation
-- handed to the kernel is the word that made this thread wait, so a release changing anything at all
-- makes it look again.
pub fn lockRead(self: RwMutex) -> {}:
    while !tryLockRead(self):
        let observed = self.state.load(LoadRelaxed)
        if (observed `and` readerBlocked) != 0:
            futexWait(self.state, observed)

{-
   Give a read lock back.

   The decrement has to answer what was there, because only the **last** reader out has anything to
   wake - and only if a writer is waiting. Every other reader's release is one atomic instruction and
   no system call, which is what makes the read path worth having at all.
-}
pub fn unlockRead(self: RwMutex) -> {}:
    let previous = self.state.fetchSub(1, UpdateRelease)

    if (previous `and` readerMask) == 1 && (previous `and` writerWaiting) != 0:
        futexWake(self.state, 0x7FFFFFFF)

{-
   One write attempt, which takes the lock only when the word is **completely** free - no writer, no
   announced writer, and no readers.

   Barging past an announced writer would be correct and would undo the preference the announcement
   exists to create, so the public attempt declines. `takeWrite` below is the one that does not, and
   it is private for exactly that reason: the only caller entitled to ignore the bit is a writer that
   is itself waiting behind it.
-}
pub fn tryLockWrite(self: RwMutex) -> Bool =
    match self.state.compareExchange(0, writerHeld, UpdateAcquire):
        Exchanged -> True
        Unchanged(->_) -> False

-- Take it from a word that has neither a holder nor a reader, whatever its announcement bit says.
-- Taking it clears that bit, and any other queued writer announces itself again.
fn takeWrite(self: RwMutex, observed: U32) -> Bool =
    match self.state.compareExchange(observed, writerHeld, UpdateAcquire):
        Exchanged -> True
        Unchanged(->_) -> False

{-
   Take it for writing.

   Three cases per turn, and the first is the one that took a deadlock to get right. A writer that
   cannot take the lock announces itself by setting bit 30, which stops new readers so the readers
   already inside can drain. But the bit is part of the word, so a writer that then re-tries
   `tryLockWrite` - which demands **zero** - can never succeed: it is blocked by its own
   announcement, and so is every other writer, forever. Nothing else in the lock is wrong and it
   still hangs.

   So the attempt here is against "no holder and no readers" rather than against zero, and the
   announcement bit is simply overwritten by the acquisition. That is also why `takeWrite` is not
   `tryLockWrite`: the public form must not barge past an announcement, and this one *is* the
   announcement.

   Setting the bit is a compare-exchange rather than a bitwise `or` because the word may have become
   free in between, and this thread would rather take the lock than say it wants it.

   The park's expectation is the word with the bit set, which is what the word is after the
   announcement - so a release, a reader leaving, or another writer arriving all make the kernel
   refuse and this thread look again.
-}
pub fn lockWrite(self: RwMutex) -> {}:
    while True:
        let observed = self.state.load(LoadRelaxed)

        if (observed `and` writerHeld) == 0 && (observed `and` readerMask) == 0:
            if takeWrite(self, observed): return
        else:
            if (observed `and` writerWaiting) == 0:
                self.state.compareExchange(observed, observed `or` writerWaiting, UpdateRelaxed)
            else:
                futexWait(self.state, observed)

{-
   Give a write lock back, to everybody.

   The store is to **zero**, which clears the waiting bit along with the held one - so a writer still
   queued has to announce itself again. That is deliberate: leaving the bit set would keep readers out
   on behalf of a writer that may have given up, and re-announcing costs one compare-exchange in a
   path that is about to make a system call anyway.

   Everyone is woken, because a write release makes progress possible for every reader at once and
   for one writer. Which of them wins is the kernel's to decide, and the losers re-check.
-}
pub fn unlockWrite(self: RwMutex) -> {}:
    self.state.store(0, StoreRelease)
    futexWake(self.state, 0x7FFFFFFF)

-- The two scoped forms, which is what almost every caller should write. Lenses for `withLock`'s
-- reason: the release runs on every way out of the block, including a `return` from inside it.
pub lens fn withRead(self: RwMutex) -> {}:
    lockRead(self)
    yield {}
    unlockRead(self)

pub lens fn withWrite(self: RwMutex) -> {}:
    lockWrite(self)
    yield {}
    unlockWrite(self)
